// // 3. Why this is much faster

// // Old system:

// // for all blocks in world

// // New system:

// // getNearby(playerChunk)
// // for ~20 blocks

// // So instead of checking 10,000 blocks, you check maybe 20–40.

// // That’s a 500× improvement.

// // 4. Why swept is better

// // Your previous system:

// // move
// // detect overlap
// // push out

// // Swept system:

// // predict collision
// // stop before impact
// // slide

// // So even at very high speeds, the player cannot tunnel through walls.

// // 5. This also prepares you for slopes

// // Later you can replace block tests with:

// // ray vs triangle
// // ray vs sphere
// // ray vs capsule

// // Same solver.

// // That’s why engines evolve toward this structure.

// // 6. Important next step (for smooth sliding)

// // Right now we only resolve one collision.

// // Eventually you’ll want:

// // max 4 collision iterations per frame

// // That lets the player:

// // run into wall
// // slide
// // hit floor
// // continue

// // I can show that next if you want.

// // And one small mindset reminder while you’re building this: systems like this feel complicated only while parts are still unknown. Once each piece is understood—chunks, sweep, slide—they become simple building blocks you can combine however you want.